WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.400
"How to Test for Functional
Vitamin B12 Deficiency"

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.740
Two cases of young, strictly
vegetarian individuals

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:20.800
with no known vascular risk factors, yet
suffering a stroke or multiple strokes.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.720
Why? Most probably because they
weren't taking vitamin B12 supplements,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.580
which leads to high homocysteine
levels, which can attack your arteries.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.000
So those eating plant-based
failing to supplement

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.120
may increase one's risk of
both heart disease and stroke.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.410
Now vegetarians have so much
heart disease risk factor benefit

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.840
that they're still at lower risk overall,
but this may help explain

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.030
why vegetarians were
found to have more stroke.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.810
Presumably this disparity would disappear
with adequate B12 supplementation,

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:56.960
and this benefit would grow even larger.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.470
Compared with non-vegetarians,
vegetarians enjoy all these other

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.550
advantages: better cholesterol,
blood pressures, blood sugars,

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:07.540
and obesity rates. But, like
what about that stroke study?

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.840
And even among studies that show
benefits, they're not as pronounced

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:16.420
as one might expect, which may be a
result of the poor vitamin B12 status.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:21.130
Vitamin B12 deficiency may negate some
of the cardiovascular disease prevention

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.590
benefits of vegetarian diets, so
in order to further reduce the risk

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.400
of cardiovascular disease,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.840
vegetarians should be advised
to use B12 supplements.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.500
How can you determine your B12 status?

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.630
By the time you're symptomatic
with B12 deficiency, it's too late.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:43.109
And initially the symptoms can be so
subtle that you might even miss them.

00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:46.170
And well before you
develop clinical deficiency,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.420
you develop metabolic vitamin B12
deficiency: a missed opportunity

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.130
to prevent strokes, where you have enough 
B12 to avoid deficiency symptoms,

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.579
but not enough to keep
your homocysteine in check.

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:02.359
Underdiagnosis of the condition results
largely from failure to understand that

00:02:02.359 --> 00:02:08.490
a normal B12 blood level may not reflect
adequate functional B12 status.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.848
The levels of B12 in your blood
does not always represent

00:02:10.848 --> 00:02:15.220
the levels of B12 in your cells.
You can have a severe functional

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.989
deficiency of B12 even though your
blood levels are normal or even high.

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:24.459
Most physicians tend to assume that
if the B12 level in your blood is normal,

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.700
then there's no problem. But 
within the lower range of normal,

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:33.980
30% of patients could have metabolic B12
deficiency with high homocysteine levels.

00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:37.599
Measuring methylmalonic acid
levels or homocysteine directly

00:02:37.599 --> 00:02:42.200
are a more accurate reflection
of vitamin B12 functional status.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.239
Methylmalonic acid can be a
simple urine test; you're looking

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:50.189
for less than a value of 4 micrograms
per milligram of creatinine.

00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:54.610
Elevated MMA is a specific marker
of vitamin B12 deficiency,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:59.400
while homocysteine rises in 
the context of both vitamin B12

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.010
and folate deficiencies,

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:05.109
and so metabolic B12 deficiency is
defined by an elevation in MMA levels

00:03:05.109 --> 00:03:10.739
or by an elevation of homocysteine
in people getting enough folate.

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:15.689
Even without eating beans and greens,
which are packed with folate,

00:03:15.689 --> 00:03:18.450
folic acid is added to
the flour supply by law.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:24.700
And so high homocysteine levels these
days may be mostly a B12 problem.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.930
Ideally, you're looking for a
homocysteine level in your blood

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.879
down in the single digits.

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:33.569
Measured this way, the prevalence
of functional B12 deficiency

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:37.080
is dramatically higher than
previously assumed,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:43.019
like 10%-40% of the general population,
and more than 40% in vegetarians,

00:03:43.019 --> 00:03:48.640
and the majority of vegans who aren't
scrupulous about getting their B12.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.580
Some suggest that those on plant-based
diets check their vitamin B12 status

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.870
every year, but you shouldn't need to
if you're adequately supplementing.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:59.090
And evidently, there are rare
cases of vitamin B12 deficiency

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.340
that can't be picked up on any test,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.420
so better to just make
sure you're getting enough.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:07.439
Now, if you do get your
homocysteine tested

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:11.409
and it's still up in the double digits
even despite B12 supplementation,

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:17.510
I do have a suggestion in
the final videos of this series,

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.600
which we'll turn to next.

